// An `i` directly after the digits of a decimal literal is the suffix, whatever
// follows it (spec/01-lexical.md, row `suffix-boundary`). Before the suffix
// existed, `1in [1, 2]` was `1 in [1, 2]`, a valid program that printed `true`.
// Now it is the Int literal `1i` and the name `n`, a syntax error. A consumer
// that has no Int literal yet runs the program, so this probe fails today on
// every consumer and is on each expected-failure list.
//
// A static error rejects the program: a non-zero exit status, and nothing
// runs, so nothing prints (spec/README.md, Conformance). The first line
// prints `ran`, so an implementation that finds the error only when it runs
// the program prints `ran` and fails this probe.

print "ran";
print 1in [1, 2];

// STATIC-ERROR
// STATUS: error
// COVERS: literal suffix-boundary
